# Internet Culture Hub — Media Architecture Rules

These rules apply to **all** AI-generated content in `lib/content/`.  
They are enforced to keep the media system consistent and production-ready.

---

## THE ONE SYSTEM: `media?: MediaItem[]`

- **Never use** `imageUrl`, `thumbnailUrl`, or `mediaEmbeds` fields.
- **Always use** `media?: MediaItem[]` for all media content.
- No other media field will ever be added to `BaseEntry`.

---

## MediaItem role rules

Every `MediaItem` must have a `role` field. Role controls placement:

| Role | Placement |
|------|-----------|
| `"featured"` | Article card thumbnail + hero image. ONE per article. |
| `"supporting"` | Image gallery section inside the article. |
| `"video"` | Video section inside the gallery. |
| `"reference"` | Link card at the bottom of the gallery. |

**Maximum one `role:"featured"` image or gif per article.**  
If you have two candidate images, pick the most representative one; set the other to `role:"supporting"`.

---

## Required fields on every MediaItem

Every `MediaItem` **must** include all of the following:

```typescript
{
  role: "featured" | "supporting" | "video" | "reference",
  type: "image" | "gif" | "video" | "embed",
  url: "",        // Direct, working URL to the actual media file or page
  title: "",      // Short human-readable title
  source: "",     // Name of the source (e.g. "Wikimedia Commons", "YouTube / Channel")
  sourceUrl: "",  // URL of the source PAGE (not the raw file URL)
  platform: "",   // One of: youtube | wikimedia | knowyourmeme | original | other
  attribution: "", // Who to credit
  verified: false, // MUST be false for AI-suggested items — humans confirm and set true
}
```

Optional but strongly recommended: `license`, `description`, `date`, `tags`.

---

## URL rules

### Verified sources (use these)
- **Wikimedia Commons**: `https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/HASH/HASH/Filename.jpg`
  - Use the direct full-file URL — NEVER the `/thumb/` CDN path.
- **YouTube thumbnails**: `https://i.ytimg.com/vi/VIDEO_ID/maxresdefault.jpg`
  - Hotlink-safe, stable YouTube CDN. Use for video-defined memes/events.
- **YouTube videos**: `https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VIDEO_ID`

### Forbidden sources
- Google Images, image search thumbnails
- Instagram, TikTok, Twitter/X media CDN (hotlink-blocked, short-lived)
- Wikimedia `/thumb/` CDN paths
- Unofficial reposts or watermarked screenshots
- Random image hosts with no clear licensing

---

## `verified` field rules

- AI-suggested media: **always** set `verified: false`
- Human has opened and confirmed the URL loads the correct image: set `verified: true`
- Never set `verified: true` in AI-generated content
- The audit script (`npm run audit:media`) will warn on unverified items

---

## Category-specific media rules

### Memes
- Featured image required if the meme is visually defined (Doge, Pepe, Nyan Cat)
- Gradient fallback acceptable for abstract or text-only memes
- Videos go in `role:"video"`, not as the featured item
- Know Your Meme and Wikipedia links go in `role:"reference"`

### Creators
- Featured photo strongly recommended — prefer Wikimedia Commons CC-licensed photos
- Never invent or guess creator photos — if no CC-licensed source exists, leave without media
- A reference to their main channel is helpful as `role:"reference"`

### Events
- Featured image of the defining moment (Wikimedia CC or YouTube thumbnail)
- Video item if a specific video defined the event
- Gradient fallback is acceptable if no CC-licensed image can be found

### Trends
- Most trends do NOT need media — gradient fallback is fine
- Only add media if the trend has a distinct visual identity

### Slang
- Almost never needs media
- Only add media if a specific visual directly explains the term's origin

---

## After adding an article

Run:
```bash
npm run audit:media
```

The audit will warn about:
- Missing featured media
- Unverified media items
- Missing required fields
- Duplicate URLs
- Multiple featured images

Fix all warnings before submitting the article.

---

## AI workflow for future media suggestions

When suggesting media for an article:
1. Identify the most representative media for each role.
2. Set `verified: false` on every item.
3. Add a comment explaining why each URL was chosen.
4. A human will verify each URL and update `verified: true`.

```typescript
// AI suggested — human must verify URL and set verified: true
{
  role: "featured",
  type: "image",
  url: "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/.../Filename.jpg",
  // ... other fields ...
  verified: false,
}
```
